The Challenge
You're given a PDF: Financial_Report_for_ABC_Labs.pdf. It contains a "Cost Benefit Analysis" table where certain cells appear blacked out. The hint — "How can you be sure of the redaction?" — is the key: you can't. The "redaction" is cosmetic only.

Why the Redaction Fails
A PDF is a layered document format.  When someone "redacts" text by drawing a black rectangle over it (e.g., in MS Word or a basic PDF editor), they're adding a visual overlay — a filled rectangle object — on top of the original text.  The text itself remains fully intact in the PDF's content stream. It's like taping a black piece of paper over a word on a page; the word is still underneath. 

This is fundamentally different from true redaction, which:

Removes the text object from the content stream
Flattens the page so the removed area becomes blank
Produces a new document where the data no longer exists in the file 
How to Solve It (Multiple Methods)
Method 1: Copy-Paste (Easiest)
Open the PDF in any viewer (Chrome, Adobe Reader, etc.), press Ctrl+A to select all text, then Ctrl+C and paste into a plain text editor. The "hidden" text appears.

Method 2: pdftotext

# Install if needed
sudo apt install poppler-utils

# Extract all text
pdftotext Financial_Report_for_ABC_Labs.pdf

# Grep for the flag
grep -oE "picoCTF\{.*\}" Financial_Report_for_ABC_Labs.txt

Method 3: Open in a Word Processor
Open the PDF in LibreOffice Draw or MS Word. The black rectangles become movable objects — drag them away to reveal the text underneath. 

Method 4: Inspect the Raw PDF
Open the PDF in a text editor or use a tool like qpdf --qdf to decompress the content stream. You'll see the text operators (e.g., Tj or TJ) for the "redacted" strings still present in the file.

